    "q-333614": {
      "dom_id": "step-q333614",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-x(t)-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=x(t)-3y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear differential equations above, where the system's matrix has repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda =-2$ of multiplicity two. Select the correct options below regarding the given system.$\\,$ The equilibrium point (origin) is a \n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nnode.",
      "worked_solution": "The matrix of our system is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that:\n\n- Since the only (repeated) eigenvalue of $A$ is negative, we have a $\\text{stable}$ node.\n\n- Since $A$ is not diagonal and has a repeated eigenvalue, there is only one linearly independent eigenvector. So, we have a $\\text{degenerate}$ node.\n\n**Note:** The phase portrait of the system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14124655/assets/c1d7f49dfe79bcd36394e4b0bb49180167ac7a8791555f0f6974f5fe49fa249f.png)",
